/* Generated by tools/build_lines.py from picks/per.txt. Every line is from: NCERT XI (2026-27) Unit 3. Do not edit by hand. */
(function () {
  const R = window.RES;
  R.addLines("per", "NCERT XI (2026-27) Unit 3", [{"sec": "Genesis of periodic classification","ids": [40,41],"t": "Mendeleev's Periodic Law: properties of elements are a periodic function of their **atomic weights**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 3,"o": "While Dobereiner initiated the study of periodic relationship, it was Mendeleev who was responsible for publishing the Periodic Law for the first time. It states as follows: The properties of the elements are a periodic function of their atomic weights."},{"sec": "Genesis of periodic classification","ids": [50,51],"t": "Mendeleev left gaps below Al and Si: **eka-aluminium = Ga**, **eka-silicon = Ge**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 3,"o": "For example, both gallium and germanium were unknown at the time Mendeleev published his Periodic Table. He left the gap under aluminium and a gap under silicon, and called these elements Eka-Aluminium and Eka-Silicon."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [61,62],"t": "√ν (X-ray frequency) vs atomic number Z is a straight line; √ν vs atomic mass is not.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "In 1913, the English physicist, Henry Moseley observed regularities in the characteristic X-ray spectra of the elements. A plot of √ν (where ν is frequency of X-rays emitted) against atomic number (Z) gave a straight line and not the plot of √ν vs atomic mass."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [63],"t": "So atomic number is more fundamental than atomic mass.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "He thereby showed that the atomic number is a more fundamental property of an element than its atomic mass."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [65],"t": "**Modern Periodic Law**: physical and chemical properties are periodic functions of **atomic number**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "This is known as the Modern Periodic Law and can be stated as: The physical and chemical properties of the elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [70],"t": "Periodic Law is a consequence of the periodic variation in electronic configuration.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "In fact, it is now recognized that the Periodic Law is essentially the consequence of the periodic variation in electronic configurations, which indeed determine the physical and chemical properties of elements and their compounds."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [76],"t": "Groups are numbered 1–18 (replacing IA…VIIA, VIII, IB…VIIB, 0).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "According to the recommendation of International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC), the groups are numbered from 1 to 18 replacing the older notation of groups IA … VIIA, VIII, IB … VIIB and 0."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [77,78],"t": "7 periods; period number = highest principal quantum number n.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "There are altogether seven periods. The period number corresponds to the highest principal quantum number (n) of the elements in the period."},{"sec": "Modern periodic law","ids": [79,80,81],"t": "Period lengths: 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32; period 7 incomplete, theoretical max 32.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 5,"o": "The first period contains 2 elements. The subsequent periods consists of 8, 8, 18, 18 and 32 elements, respectively. The seventh period is incomplete and like the sixth period would have a theoretical maximum (on the basis of quantum numbers) of 32 elements."},{"sec": "Nomenclature of elements with Z > 100","ids": [97],"t": "Z > 100, until officially named: systematic name from numerical roots for 0–9 of the atomic number.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 7,"o": "The IUPAC has made recommendation that until a new element’s discovery is proved, and its name is officially recognised, a systematic nomenclature be derived directly from the atomic number of the element using the numerical roots for 0 and numbers 1-9."},{"sec": "Nomenclature of elements with Z > 100","ids": [99],"t": "Roots in order of the digits, then add \"-ium\".","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 7,"o": "The roots are put together in order of digits which make up the atomic number and “ium” is added at the end."},{"sec": "Nomenclature of elements with Z > 100","ids": [101],"t": "Table 3.5: 101 Unnilunium (Unu), 102 Unnilbium (Unb), 103 Unniltrium (Unt), 104 Unnilquadium (Unq), 105 Unnilpentium (Unp), 106 Unnilhexium (Unh), 107 Unnilseptium (Uns), 108 Unniloctium (Uno), 109 Unnilennium (Une), 110 Ununnillium (Uun), 111 Unununnium (Uuu), 112 Ununbium (Uub).","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "per","page": 7,"o": "Table 3.5: 101 Unnilunium (Unu), 102 Unnilbium (Unb), 103 Unniltrium (Unt), 104 Unnilquadium (Unq), 105 Unnilpentium (Unp), 106 Unnilhexium (Unh), 107 Unnilseptium (Uns), 108 Unniloctium (Uno), 109 Unnilennium (Une), 110 Ununnillium (Uun), 111 Unununnium (Uuu), 112 Ununbium (Uub)."},{"sec": "Nomenclature of elements with Z > 100","ids": [102],"t": "Temporary symbol has three letters.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "Thus, the new element first gets a temporary name, with symbol consisting of three letters."},{"sec": "Nomenclature of elements with Z > 100","ids": [106,107],"t": "Z = 120: roots un (1), bi (2), nil (0) → **Ubn, unbinilium**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "From Table 3.4, the roots for 1, 2 and 0 are un, bi and nil, respectively. Hence, the symbol and the name respectively are Ubn and unbinilium."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [109,110],"t": "An element's position reflects the quantum numbers of the last orbital filled.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "The distribution of electrons into orbitals of an atom is called its electronic configuration. An element’s location in the Periodic Table reflects the quantum numbers of the last orbital filled."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [112,113],"t": "Period number = n of the valence shell; each new period starts filling the next principal level.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "(a) Electronic Configurations in Periods The period indicates the value of n for the outermost or valence shell. In other words, successive period in the Periodic Table is associated with the filling of the next higher principal energy level (n = 1, n = 2, etc.)."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [114],"t": "Elements in a period = 2 × number of orbitals in the level being filled.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "It can be readily seen that the number of elements in each period is twice the number of atomic orbitals available in the energy level that is being filled."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [115,116,117,118],"t": "Period 1 (n = 1): 1s, so H, He. Period 2: Li (2s) to Ne (2s^2 2p^6), 8 elements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "The first period (n = 1) starts with the filling of the lowest level (1s) and therefore has two elements — hydrogen (ls1 ) and helium (ls2 ) when the first shell (K) is completed. The second period (n = 2) starts with lithium and the third electron enters the 2s orbital. The next element, beryllium has four electrons and has the electronic configuration 1s2 2s2 . Starting from the next element boron, the 2p orbitals are filled with electrons when the L shell is completed at neon (2s2 2p6 ). Thus there are 8 elements in the second period."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [119],"t": "Period 3: Na to Ar (3s, 3p), 8 elements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "The third period (n = 3) begins at sodium, and the added electron enters a 3s orbital. Successive filling of 3s and 3p orbitals gives rise to the third period of 8 elements from sodium to argon."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [120],"t": "Before 4p fills, 3d filling becomes energetically favourable, giving the 3d transition series.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "Now you may note that before the 4p orbital is filled, filling up of 3d orbitals becomes energetically favourable and we come across the so called 3d transition series of elements."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [121,122,123,124],"t": "Period 4: 3d series from Sc (3d^1 4s^2) to Zn (3d^10 4s^2); ends at Kr (4p); 18 elements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "This starts from scandium (Z = 21) which has the electronic configuration 3d1 4s2 . The 3d orbitals are filled at zinc (Z=30) with electronic configuration 3d10 4s2 . The fourth period ends at krypton with the filling up of the 4p orbitals. Altogether we have 18 elements in this fourth period."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [125,126],"t": "Period 5: from Rb; 4d series from Y (Z = 39); ends at Xe (5p).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "The fifth period (n = 5) beginning with rubidium is similar to the fourth period and contains the 4d transition series starting at yttrium (Z = 39). This period ends at xenon with the filling up of the 5p orbitals."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [127],"t": "Period 6: 32 elements (6s, 4f, 5d, 6p); 4f filling from Ce (58) to Lu (71) gives the lanthanoids.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "The sixth period (n = 6) contains 32 elements and successive electrons enter 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p orbitals, in the order — filling up of the 4f orbitals begins with cerium (Z = 58) and ends at lutetium (Z = 71) to give the 4f-inner transition series which is called the lanthanoid series."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [128],"t": "Period 7: 7s, 5f, 6d, 7p; mostly man-made radioactive elements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "The seventh period (n = 7) is similar to the sixth period with the successive filling up of the 7s, 5f, 6d and 7p orbitals and includes most of the man-made radioactive elements."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [129],"t": "Period 7 ends at Z = 118 (noble gas).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "This period will end at the element with atomic number 118 which would belong to the noble gas family."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [130,131],"t": "5f filling after Ac (Z = 89) gives the actinoids.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 8,"o": "Filling up of the 5f orbitals after actinium (Z = 89) gives the 5f-inner transition series known as the actinoid series."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [132],"t": "4f and 5f series are placed separately to keep elements with similar properties in one column.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "The 4f- and 5f-inner transition series of elements are placed separately in the Periodic Table to maintain its structure and to preserve the principle of classification by keeping elements with similar properties in a single column."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [135,136,137],"t": "Period 5: energy order 5s < 4d < 5p; 9 orbitals, so 18 electrons and 18 elements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "The order in which the energy of the available orbitals 4d, 5s and 5p increases is 5s < 4d < 5p. The total number of orbitals available are 9. The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated is 18; and therefore 18 elements are there in the 5th period."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [138],"t": "Same group: similar valence-shell configuration, same number of outer electrons, similar properties.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "(b) Groupwise Electronic Configurations Elements in the same vertical column or group have similar valence shell electronic configurations, the same number of electrons in the outer orbitals, and similar properties."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [142],"t": "Four blocks (s, p, d, f) by the type of orbital being filled.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "We can classify the elements into four blocks viz., s-block, p-block, d-block and f-block depending on the type of atomic orbitals that are being filled with electrons."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [145],"t": "He is strictly s-block but sits in p-block (group 18): completely filled valence shell 1s^2, noble-gas properties.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "Strictly, helium belongs to the s-block but its positioning in the p-block along with other group 18 elements is justified because it has a completely filled valence shell (1s^2) and as a result, exhibits properties characteristic of other noble gases."},{"sec": "Electronic configurations and the periodic table","ids": [146,147,148],"t": "H: one s-electron, so it fits group 1; it can also gain one electron, so it can act like group 17.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "The other exception is hydrogen. It has only one s-electron and hence can be placed in group 1 (alkali metals). It can also gain an electron to achieve a noble gas arrangement and hence it can behave similar to a group 17 (halogen family) elements."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [153],"t": "s-block: groups 1 (ns^1) and 2 (ns^2).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 9,"o": "The elements of Group 1 (alkali metals) and Group 2 (alkaline earth metals) which have ns^1 and ns^2 outermost electronic configuration belong to the s-Block Elements."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [158,159],"t": "s-block: reactive metals, low ionization enthalpies; form M^+ (group 1) or M^2+ (group 2).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "They are all reactive metals with low ionization enthalpies. They lose the outermost electron(s) readily to form 1+ ion (in the case of alkali metals) or 2+ ion (in the case of alkaline earth metals)."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [160,161],"t": "s-block: metallic character and reactivity increase down the group; never found free in nature.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The metallic character and the reactivity increase as we go down the group. Because of high reactivity they are never found pure in nature."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [162],"t": "s-block compounds are predominantly ionic, **except those of Li and Be**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The compounds of the s-block elements, with the exception of those of lithium and beryllium are predominantly ionic."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [163,164],"t": "p-block = groups 13–18; s + p together = representative (main group) elements; outer configuration ns^2np^1 to ns^2np^6.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The p-Block Elements comprise those belonging to Group 13 to 18 and these together with the s-Block Elements are called the Representative Elements or Main Group Elements. The outermost electronic configuration varies from ns^2np^1 to ns^2np^6 in each period."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [165,166,167],"t": "Each period ends with a noble gas (ns^2np^6); its filled valence shell is hard to alter, so very low reactivity.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "At the end of each period is a noble gas element with a closed valence shell ns2 np6 configuration. All the orbitals in the valence shell of the noble gases are completely filled by electrons and it is very difficult to alter this stable arrangement by the addition or removal of electrons. The noble gases thus exhibit very low chemical reactivity."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [169,170],"t": "Halogens (17) and chalcogens (16) have highly negative electron gain enthalpies; they add one or two electrons to reach noble-gas configuration.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "They are the halogens (Group 17) and the chalcogens (Group 16). These two groups of elements have highly negative electron gain enthalpies and readily add one or two electrons respectively to attain the stable noble gas configuration."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [171],"t": "Across a period non-metallic character increases; down a group metallic character increases. d-block = groups 3–12.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The non-metallic character increases as we move from left to right across a period and metallic character increases as we go down the group. 3.6.3 The d-Block Elements (Transition Elements) These are the elements of Group 3 to 12 in the centre of the Periodic Table."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [172],"t": "d-block: inner d orbitals are being filled.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "These are characterised by the filling of inner d orbitals by electrons and are therefore referred to as d-Block Elements."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [173],"t": "d-block outer configuration (n-1)d^1-10 ns^0-2; **exception Pd: 4d^10 5s^0**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "These elements have the general outer electronic configuration (n-1)d^1-10 ns^0-2 except for Pd where its electronic configuration is 4d^10 5s^0."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [175,176],"t": "d-block: coloured ions, variable oxidation states, paramagnetism, catalysis. **Zn, Cd, Hg ((n-1)d^10 ns^2) show few of these properties.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "They mostly form coloured ions, exhibit variable valence (oxidation states), paramagnetism and oftenly used as catalysts. However, Zn, Cd and Hg which have the electronic configuration, (n-1)d^10 ns^2 do not show most of the properties of transition elements."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [177],"t": "Transition metals bridge the reactive s-block metals and the less active groups 13 and 14.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "In a way, transition metals form a bridge between the chemically active metals of s-block elements and the less active elements of Groups 13 and 14 and thus take their familiar name “Transition Elements”."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [178],"t": "Lanthanoids Ce (58)–Lu (71), actinoids Th (90)–Lr (103): outer configuration (n-2)f^1-14 (n-1)d^0–1 ns^2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The two rows of elements at the bottom of the Periodic Table, called the Lanthanoids, Ce(Z = 58) – Lu(Z = 71) and Actinoids, Th(Z = 90) – Lr (Z = 103) are characterised by the outer electronic configuration (n-2)f^1-14 (n-1)d^0–1 ns^2."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [179],"t": "f-block (inner transition): the last electron enters an f orbital.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The last electron added to each element is filled in f-orbital. These two series of elements are hence called the InnerTransition Elements (f-Block Elements)."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [181],"t": "Within each f-series properties are very similar.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "Within each series, the properties of the elements are quite similar."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [182,183],"t": "Early actinoids: more complicated than lanthanoids because of many oxidation states; actinoids are radioactive.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The chemistry of the early actinoids is more complicated than the corresponding lanthanoids, due to the large number of oxidation states possible for these actinoid elements. Actinoid elements are radioactive."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [185],"t": "Elements after uranium = transuranium elements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The elements after uranium are called Transuranium Elements."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [188],"t": "Z = 117: group 17, [Rn] 5f^14 6d^10 7s^2 7p^5.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "Element with Z = 117, would belong to the halogen family (Group 17) and the electronic configuration would be [Rn] 5f^14 6d^10 7s^2 7p^5."},{"sec": "s-, p-, d-, f-blocks","ids": [189],"t": "Z = 120: group 2, [Uuo] 8s^2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 11,"o": "The element with Z = 120, will be placed in Group 2 (alkaline earth metals), and will have the electronic configuration [Uuo]8s^2."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [193],"t": "Metals: more than 78% of elements, on the left of the table.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "Metals comprise more than 78% of all known elements and appear on the left side of the Periodic Table."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [194],"t": "Metals are solids at room temperature, **except Hg**; Ga (303 K) and Cs (302 K) melt very low.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "Metals are usually solids at room temperature [mercury is an exception; gallium and caesium also have very low melting points (303K and 302K, respectively)]."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [195,196,197],"t": "Metals: high m.p./b.p., good conductors, malleable, ductile.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "Metals usually have high melting and boiling points. They are good conductors of heat and electricity. They are malleable (can be flattened into thin sheets by hammering) and ductile (can be drawn into wires)."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [198,199],"t": "Non-metals: top right; along a period character changes from metallic (left) to non-metallic (right).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "In contrast, non-metals are located at the top right hand side of the Periodic Table. In fact, in a horizontal row, the property of elements change from metallic on the left to non-metallic on the right."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [200],"t": "Non-metals: solids or gases with low m.p./b.p., **except B and C**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "Non-metals are usually solids or gases at room temperature with low melting and boiling points (boron and carbon are exceptions)."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [201,202],"t": "Non-metals: poor conductors; solids brittle, neither malleable nor ductile.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "They are poor conductors of heat and electricity. Most nonmetallic solids are brittle and are neither malleable nor ductile."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [203,204],"t": "More metallic down a group, more non-metallic left to right; the change is gradual (zig-zag line).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "The elements become more metallic as we go down a group; the non-metallic character increases as one goes from left to right across the Periodic Table. The change from metallic to non-metallic character is not abrupt as shown by the thick zig-zag line in Fig. 3.3."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [205,206],"t": "Metalloids (along the zig-zag): Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "The elements (e.g., silicon, germanium, arsenic, antimony and tellurium) bordering this line and running diagonally across the Periodic Table show properties that are characteristic of both metals and non-metals. These elements are called Semi-metals or Metalloids."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [208],"t": "Increasing metallic character: P < Si < Be < Mg < Na.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "Hence the order of increasing metallic character is: P < Si < Be < Mg < Na."},{"sec": "Metals, non-metals and metalloids","ids": [209,210],"t": "Reactivity in a period: high at group 1, low in the middle, maximum at group 17. Down a group: increases for alkali metals, decreases for halogens.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 12,"o": "For example, within a period, chemical reactivity tends to be high in Group 1 metals, lower in elements towards the middle of the table, and increases to a maximum in the Group 17 non-metals. Likewise within a group of representative metals (say alkali metals) reactivity increases on moving down the group, whereas within a group of non-metals (say halogens), reactivity decreases down the group."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [220,221,222],"t": "Atomic size can't be measured exactly (no sharp boundary); it is estimated from interatomic distances in the combined state.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "Secondly, since the electron cloud surrounding the atom does not have a sharp boundary, the determination of the atomic size cannot be precise. In other words, there is no practical way by which the size of an individual atom can be measured. However, an estimate of the atomic size can be made by knowing the distance between the atoms in the combined state."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [223,224],"t": "Covalent radius = half the single-bond distance; Cl2 bond 198 pm, so r(Cl) = 99 pm.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "One practical approach to estimate the size of an atom of a non-metallic element is to measure the distance between two atoms when they are bound together by a single bond in a covalent molecule and from this value, the “Covalent Radius” of the element can be calculated. For example, the bond distance in the chlorine molecule (Cl2) is 198 pm and half this distance (99 pm), is taken as the atomic radius of chlorine."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [225],"t": "Metallic radius = half the internuclear distance in the metal crystal; Cu–Cu 256 pm, so r(Cu) = 128 pm.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "For metals, we define the term “Metallic Radius” which is taken as half the internuclear distance separating the metal cores in the metallic crystal. For example, the distance between two adjacent copper atoms in solid copper is 256 pm; hence the metallic radius of copper is assigned a value of 128 pm."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [226],"t": "\"Atomic radius\" means covalent radius for non-metals and metallic radius for metals; measured by X-ray or spectroscopic methods.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "For simplicity, in this book, we use the term Atomic Radius to refer to both covalent or metallic radius depending on whether the element is a non-metal or a metal. Atomic radii can be measured by X-ray or other spectroscopic methods."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [230,231],"t": "Atomic size decreases across a period: same valence shell, rising effective nuclear charge.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "The atomic size generally decreases across a period. It is because within the period the outer electrons are in the same valence shell and the effective nuclear charge increases as the atomic number increases resulting in the increased attraction of electrons to the nucleus."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [232],"t": "Atomic radius increases down a group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "Within a family or vertical column of the periodic table, the atomic radius increases regularly with atomic number."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [233,234,235],"t": "Down groups 1 and 17: n increases and filled inner levels shield the valence electrons, so size increases.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "For alkali metals and halogens, as we descend the groups, the principal quantum number (n) increases and the valence electrons are farther from the nucleus. This happens because the inner energy levels are filled with electrons, which serve to shield the outer electrons from the pull of the nucleus. Consequently the size of the atom increases as reflected in the atomic radii."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [236,237,238],"t": "Noble-gas radii are non-bonded (van der Waals) radii, very large; compare them with van der Waals radii, not covalent radii.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "Note that the atomic radii of noble gases are not considered here. Being monoatomic, their (non-bonded radii) values are very large. In fact radii of noble gases should be compared not with the covalent radii but with the van der Waals radii of other elements."},{"sec": "Atomic radius","ids": [239],"t": "Table 3.6: Period II Li 152, Be 111, B 88, C 77, N 74, O 66, F 64 pm; Period III Na 186, Mg 160, Al 143, Si 117, P 110, S 104, Cl 99 pm; Group 1 Li 152, Na 186, K 231, Rb 244, Cs 262; Group 17 F 64, Cl 99, Br 114, I 133, At 140 pm.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "per","page": 13,"o": "Table 3.6: Period II Li 152, Be 111, B 88, C 77, N 74, O 66, F 64 pm; Period III Na 186, Mg 160, Al 143, Si 117, P 110, S 104, Cl 99 pm; Group 1 Li 152, Na 186, K 231, Rb 244, Cs 262; Group 17 F 64, Cl 99, Br 114, I 133, At 140 pm."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [240,241,242],"t": "Ionic radii come from cation–anion distances in ionic crystals; they follow the same trends as atomic radii.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "(b) Ionic Radius The removal of an electron from an atom results in the formation of a cation, whereas gain of an electron leads to an anion. The ionic radii can be estimated by measuring the distances between cations and anions in ionic crystals. In general, the ionic radii of elements exhibit the same trend as the atomic radii."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [243],"t": "Cation < parent atom: fewer electrons, same nuclear charge.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "A cation is smaller than its parent atom because it has fewer electrons while its nuclear charge remains the same."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [244],"t": "Anion > parent atom: added electrons increase repulsion and lower the effective nuclear charge.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "The size of an anion will be larger than that of the parent atom because the addition of one or more electrons would result in increased repulsion among the electrons and a decrease in effective nuclear charge."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [245,246],"t": "F^– 136 pm vs F 64 pm; Na 186 pm vs Na^+ 95 pm.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "For example, the ionic radius of fluoride ion (F^–) is 136 pm whereas the atomic radius of fluorine is only 64 pm. On the other hand, the atomic radius of sodium is 186 pm compared to the ionic radius of 95 pm for Na^+."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [247,248,249],"t": "Isoelectronic species (e.g. O^2–, F^–, Na^+, Mg^2+, all 10 e^–) differ in radius because of different nuclear charge.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "When we find some atoms and ions which contain the same number of electrons, we call them isoelectronic species. For example, O^2–, F^–, Na^+ and Mg^2+ have the same number of electrons (10). Their radii would be different because of their different nuclear charges."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [250,251],"t": "Isoelectronic: higher positive charge, smaller radius; higher negative charge, larger radius.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "The cation with the greater positive charge will have a smaller radius because of the greater attraction of the electrons to the nucleus. Anion with the greater negative charge will have the larger radius."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [255,256,257],"t": "Summary: radii decrease across a period; cations < parent atoms; among isoelectronic species, larger nuclear charge means smaller radius.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "Atomic radii decrease across a period. Cations are smaller than their parent atoms. Among isoelectronic species, the one with the larger positive nuclear charge will have a smaller radius."},{"sec": "Ionic radius","ids": [258],"t": "Of Mg, Mg^2+, Al, Al^3+: largest Mg, smallest Al^3+.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "Hence the largest species is Mg; the smallest one is Al^3+."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [259],"t": "Ionization enthalpy measures the tendency of an element to lose an electron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "(c) Ionization Enthalpy A quantitative measure of the tendency of an element to lose electron is given by its Ionization Enthalpy."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [260],"t": "Energy to remove an electron from an isolated gaseous atom in its ground state.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 14,"o": "It represents the energy required to remove an electron from an isolated gaseous atom (X) in its ground state."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [262,263],"t": "∆iH1: X(g) → X^+(g) + e^–, in kJ mol^–1.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "88 chemistry In other words, the first ionization enthalpy for an element X is the enthalpy change (∆i H) for the reaction depicted in equation 3.1. X(g) → X+ (g) + e– (3.1) The ionization enthalpy is expressed in units of kJ mol–1 ."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [264],"t": "∆iH2: energy to remove the second most loosely bound electron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "We can define the second ionization enthalpy as the energy required to remove the second most loosely bound electron; it is the energy required to carry out the reaction shown in equation 3.2."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [265,266],"t": "Ionization enthalpies are always positive; ∆iH2 > ∆iH1 (removing from a positive ion is harder).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "Energy is always required to remove electrons from an atom and hence ionization enthalpies are always positive. The second ionization enthalpy will be higher than the first ionization enthalpy because it is more difficult to remove an electron from a positively charged ion than from a neutral atom."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [267,268],"t": "∆iH3 > ∆iH2 and so on; unqualified \"ionization enthalpy\" means the first.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "In the same way the third ionization enthalpy will be higher than the second and so on. The term “ionization enthalpy”, if not qualified, is taken as the first ionization enthalpy."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [271,272],"t": "∆iH maxima at noble gases (closed shells), minima at alkali metals (hence their high reactivity).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "You will find maxima at the noble gases which have closed electron shells and very stable electron configurations. On the other hand, minima occur at the alkali metals and their low ionization enthalpies can be correlated with their high reactivity."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [273],"t": "∆iH1 generally increases across a period and decreases down a group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "The first ionization enthalpy generally increases as we go across a period and decreases as we descend in a group."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [275,276],"t": "∆iH depends on electron–nucleus attraction vs electron–electron repulsion; closely related to atomic radius.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "You will appreciate that the ionization enthalpy and atomic radius are closely related properties. To understand these trends, we have to consider two factors : (i) the attraction of electrons towards the nucleus, and (ii) the repulsion of electrons from each other."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [277,278],"t": "Effective nuclear charge < actual nuclear charge because core electrons shield the valence electron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 15,"o": "The effective nuclear charge experienced by a valence electron in an atom will be less than the actual charge on the nucleus because of “shielding” or “screening” of the valence electron from the nucleus by the intervening core electrons."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [279,280],"t": "Li: the 2s electron is shielded by 1s^2, so it feels less than +3.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "For example, the 2s electron in lithium is shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of 1s electrons. As a result, the valence electron experiences a net positive charge which is less than the actual charge of +3."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [281],"t": "Shielding is effective when inner shells are completely filled.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "In general, shielding is effective when the orbitals in the inner shells are completely filled."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [282],"t": "This applies to alkali metals: a single ns electron over a noble-gas core.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "This situation occurs in the case of alkali metals which have single outermost ns-electron preceded by a noble gas electronic configuration."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [283],"t": "Across period 2 (Li to F), electrons go into the same shell and core shielding barely increases.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "When we move from lithium to fluorine across the second period, successive electrons are added to orbitals in the same principal quantum level and the shielding of the nuclear charge by the inner core of electrons does not increase very much to compensate for the increased attraction of the electron to the nucleus."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [284,285],"t": "So across a period nuclear charge outweighs shielding, and ∆iH increases.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "Thus, across a period, increasing nuclear charge outweighs the shielding. Consequently, the outermost electrons are held more and more tightly and the ionization enthalpy increases across a period."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [286],"t": "Down a group the outer electron is farther away and more shielded by inner levels.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "As we go down a group, the outermost electron being increasingly farther from the nucleus, there is an increased shielding of the nuclear charge by the electrons in the inner levels."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [287],"t": "Down a group shielding outweighs the rising nuclear charge, so ∆iH decreases.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "In this case, increase in shielding outweighs the increasing nuclear charge and the removal of the outermost electron requires less energy down a group."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [288],"t": "**∆iH1(B) < ∆iH1(Be)**, though B has a greater nuclear charge.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "The first ionization enthalpy of boron (Z = 5) is slightly less than that of beryllium (Z = 4) even though the former has a greater nuclear charge."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [289],"t": "In the same shell an s-electron is attracted to the nucleus more than a p-electron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "When we consider the same principal quantum level, an s-electron is attracted to the nucleus more than a p-electron."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [290],"t": "Be loses a 2s electron; B loses a 2p electron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "In beryllium, the electron removed during the ionization is an s-electron whereas the electron removed during ionization of boron is a p-electron."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [291,292],"t": "2s penetrates more than 2p, so B's 2p electron is more shielded and easier to remove than Be's 2s.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "The penetration of a 2s-electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2p-electron; hence the 2p electron of boron is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons of beryllium. Therefore, it is easier to remove the 2p-electron from boron compared to the removal of a 2s-electron from beryllium."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [293],"t": "Hence ∆iH1(B) < ∆iH1(Be).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "Thus, boron has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than beryllium."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [294,295],"t": "**∆iH1(O) < ∆iH1(N)**: N's three 2p electrons are in separate orbitals (Hund), while O has one paired 2p orbital, so extra electron–electron repulsion.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "Another “anomaly” is the smaller first ionization enthalpy of oxygen compared to nitrogen. This arises because in the nitrogen atom, three 2p-electrons reside in different atomic orbitals (Hund’s rule) whereas in the oxygen atom, two of the four 2p-electrons must occupy the same 2p-orbital resulting in an increased electron-electron repulsion."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [296],"t": "So O's fourth 2p electron is easier to remove than one of N's three.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "Consequently, it is easier to remove the fourth 2p-electron from oxygen than it is, to remove one of the three 2p-electrons from nitrogen."},{"sec": "Ionization enthalpy","ids": [297,300,301],"t": "∆iH1: Na 496, Mg 737, Si 786 kJ mol^–1; Al ≈ 575, **lower than Mg**, because 3s electrons shield the 3p electron.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "The first ionization enthalpy values of the third period elements, Na, Mg and Si are respectively 496, 737 and 786 kJ mol^–1. It will be more close to 575 kJ mol^–1. The value for Al should be lower than that of Mg because of effective shielding of 3p electrons from the nucleus by 3s-electrons."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [302,303],"t": "∆egH = enthalpy change when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom to form an anion; measures ease of anion formation.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "(d) Electron Gain Enthalpy When an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom (X) to convert it into a negative ion, the enthalpy change accompanying the process is defined as the Electron Gain Enthalpy (∆eg H). Electron gain enthalpy provides a measure of the ease with which an atom adds an electron to form anion as represented by equation 3.3."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [304,305],"t": "Adding an electron can be exothermic or endothermic; for many elements energy is released (∆egH negative).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "Depending on the element, the process of adding an electron to the atom can be either endothermic or exothermic. For many elements energy is released when an electron is added to the atom and the electron gain enthalpy is negative."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [306,307],"t": "Halogens: very high negative ∆egH (one electron gives a noble-gas configuration).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 16,"o": "For example, group 17 elements (the halogens) have very high negative electron gain enthalpies because they can attain stable noble gas electronic configurations by picking up an electron."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [308],"t": "**Noble gases: large positive ∆egH**; the electron must enter the next shell.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "On the other hand, noble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpies because the electron has to enter the next higher principal quantum level leading to a very unstable electronic configuration."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [309],"t": "Most negative ∆egH: upper right of the table, just before the noble gases.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "It may be noted that electron gain enthalpies have large negative values toward the upper right of the periodic table preceding the noble gases."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [310],"t": "∆egH varies less systematically than ∆iH.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "The variation in electron gain enthalpies of elements is less systematic than for ionization enthalpies."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [311],"t": "∆egH generally becomes more negative across a period.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "As a general rule, electron gain enthalpy becomes more negative with increase in the atomic number across a period."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [312],"t": "Rising effective nuclear charge across a period makes it easier for a smaller atom to add an electron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "The effective nuclear charge increases from left to right across a period and consequently it will be easier to add an electron to a smaller atom since the added electron on an average would be closer to the positively charged nucleus."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [313],"t": "Down a group ∆egH should become less negative (larger atom, electron farther away).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "We should also expect electron gain enthalpy to become less negative as we go down a group because the size of the atom increases and the added electron would be farther from the nucleus."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [315,316,317],"t": "**∆egH of O and F is less negative than S and Cl**: the electron goes into the small n = 2 shell with strong repulsion; in n = 3 the repulsion is much less.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "However, electron gain enthalpy of O or F is less negative than that of the succeeding element. This is because when an electron is added to O or F, the added electron goes to the smaller n = 2 quantum level and suffers significant repulsion from the other electrons present in this level. For the n = 3 quantum level (S or Cl), the added electron occupies a larger region of space and the electron-electron repulsion is much less."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [320,321],"t": "Summary: ∆egH more negative across a period, less negative down a group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative across a period as we move from left to right. Within a group, electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [322],"t": "Of P, S, Cl, F: most negative ∆egH **Cl**, least negative P.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "Hence the element with most negative electron gain enthalpy is chlorine; the one with the least negative electron gain enthalpy is phosphorus."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [327,328],"t": "Electron affinity (Ae) = –∆egH; positive when energy is released (opposite to the thermodynamic sign convention).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "In many books, the negative of the enthalpy change for the process depicted in equation 3.3 is defined as the ELECTRON AFFINITY (Ae) of the atom under consideration. If energy is released when an electron is added to an atom, the electron affinity is taken as positive, contrary to thermodynamic convention."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [329],"t": "If energy must be supplied, electron affinity is negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "If energy has to be supplied to add an electron to an atom, then the electron affinity of the atom is assigned a negative sign."},{"sec": "Electron gain enthalpy","ids": [330],"t": "Table 3.7 (kJ mol^–1): H –73, Li –60, Na –53, K –48; O –141, S –200, Se –195; F –328, Cl –349, Br –325; He +48, Ne +116, Ar +96, Kr +96.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "Table 3.7 (kJ mol^–1): H –73, Li –60, Na –53, K –48; O –141, S –200, Se –195; F –328, Cl –349, Br –325; He +48, Ne +116, Ar +96, Kr +96."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [323,324],"t": "Electronegativity: qualitative ability of a bonded atom to attract shared electrons; **not a measurable quantity**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "A qualitative measure of the ability of an atom in a chemical compound to attract shared electrons to itself is called electronegativity. Unlike ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy, it is not a measureable quantity."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [325,326],"t": "Scales: Pauling, Mulliken-Jaffe, Allred-Rochow; Pauling is most widely used.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "However, a number of numerical scales of electronegativity of elements viz., Pauling scale, Mulliken-Jaffe scale, Allred-Rochow scale have been developed. The one which is the most widely used is the Pauling scale."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [327,331],"t": "Pauling scale: F assigned 4.0 (greatest ability to attract electrons).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 17,"o": "Linus Pauling, an American scientist, in 1922 assigned arbitrarily a value of 4.0 to fluorine, the element considered to have the greatest ability to attract electrons."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [332],"t": "Electronegativity of an element is not constant; it depends on the bonded partner.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 18,"o": "The electronegativity of any given element is not constant; it varies depending on the element to which it is bound."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [334],"t": "EN increases across a period (Li → F) and decreases down a group (F → At).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 18,"o": "Electronegativity generally increases across a period from left to right (say from lithium to fluorine) and decrease down a group (say from fluorine to astatine) in the periodic table."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [337,338,339,340],"t": "EN rises as atomic radius falls across a period and falls as radius grows down a group; same trend as ∆iH.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 18,"o": "The attraction between the outer (or valence) electrons and the nucleus increases as the atomic radius decreases in a period. The electronegativity also increases. On the same account electronegativity values decrease with the increase in atomic radii down a group. The trend is similar to that of ionization enthalpy."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [342],"t": "Table 3.8(a): Li 1.0, Be 1.5, B 2.0, C 2.5, N 3.0, O 3.5, F 4.0; Na 0.9, Mg 1.2, Al 1.5, Si 1.8, P 2.1, S 2.5, Cl 3.0.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "per","page": 18,"o": "Table 3.8(a): Li 1.0, Be 1.5, B 2.0, C 2.5, N 3.0, O 3.5, F 4.0; Na 0.9, Mg 1.2, Al 1.5, Si 1.8, P 2.1, S 2.5, Cl 3.0."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [344,345],"t": "EN is directly related to non-metallic character and inversely to metallic character.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "Therefore, electronegativity is directly related to that non-metallic properties of elements. It can be further extended to say that the electronegativity is inversely related to the metallic properties of elements."},{"sec": "Electronegativity","ids": [346,347],"t": "So across a period: EN up, non-metallic up. Down a group: EN down, metallic up.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "Thus, the increase in electronegativities across a period is accompanied by an increase in non-metallic properties (or decrease in metallic properties) of elements. Similarly, the decrease in electronegativity down a group is accompanied by a decrease in non-metallic properties (or increase in metallic properties) of elements."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [350],"t": "Valence is the most characteristic property and follows from electronic configuration.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "(a) Periodicity of Valence or Oxidation States The valence is the most characteristic property of the elements and can be understood in terms of their electronic configurations."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [351],"t": "Valence of representative elements = number of outer electrons, or 8 minus that number (usually).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "The valence of representative elements is usually (though not necessarily) equal to the number of electrons in the outermost orbitals and/or equal to eight minus the number of outermost electrons."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [353,354,355],"t": "EN order F > O > Na. **In OF2, O is +2** (F is –1).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "Consider the two oxygen containing compounds: OF2 and Na2O. The order of electronegativity of the three elements involved in these compounds is F > O > Na. Being highest electronegative element, fluorine is given oxidation state –1. Oxygen shares two electrons with fluorine atoms and thereby exhibits oxidation state +2."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [356,357],"t": "In Na2O, O is –2 and Na (3s^1) is +1.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "In Na2 O, oxygen being more electronegative accepts two electrons, one from each of the two sodium atoms and, thus, shows oxidation state –2. On the other hand sodium with electronic configuration 3s1 loses one electron to oxygen and is given oxidation state +1."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [358],"t": "Oxidation state = charge an atom acquires on electronegativity grounds from the other atoms in the molecule.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "Thus, the oxidation state of an element in a particular compound can be defined as the charge acquired by its atom on the basis of electronegative consideration from other atoms in the molecule."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [360,361],"t": "Si (valence 4) + Br (valence 1) → SiBr4.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "(a) Silicon is group 14 element with a valence of 4; bromine belongs to the halogen family with a valence of 1. Hence the formula of the compound formed would be SiBr4 ."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [362,363],"t": "Al (valence 3) + S (valence 2) → Al2S3.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 19,"o": "(b) Aluminium belongs to group 13 with a valence of 3; sulphur belongs to group 16 elements with a valence of 2. Hence, the formula of the compound formed would be Al2 S3 ."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [367,368],"t": "Variable valence is characteristic of transition elements and actinoids.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "There are many elements which exhibit variable valence. This is particularly characteristic of transition elements and actinoids, which we shall study later."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [371],"t": "Table 3.9 hydrides: LiH, NaH, KH; CaH2; B2H6, AlH3; CH4, SiH4, GeH4, SnH4; NH3, PH3, AsH3; H2O, H2S, H2Se, H2Te; HF, HCl, HBr, HI.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "Table 3.9 hydrides: LiH, NaH, KH; CaH2; B2H6, AlH3; CH4, SiH4, GeH4, SnH4; NH3, PH3, AsH3; H2O, H2S, H2Se, H2Te; HF, HCl, HBr, HI."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [371],"t": "Table 3.9 oxides: Li2O, Na2O, K2O; MgO, CaO, SrO, BaO; B2O3, Al2O3, Ga2O3, In2O3; CO2, SiO2, GeO2, SnO2, PbO2; N2O3, N2O5; P4O6, P4O10; As2O3, As2O5; Sb2O3, Sb2O5; Bi2O3; SO3, SeO3, TeO3.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "Table 3.9 oxides: Li2O, Na2O, K2O; MgO, CaO, SrO, BaO; B2O3, Al2O3, Ga2O3, In2O3; CO2, SiO2, GeO2, SnO2, PbO2; N2O3, N2O5; P4O6, P4O10; As2O3, As2O5; Sb2O3, Sb2O5; Bi2O3; SO3, SeO3, TeO3."},{"sec": "Valence and oxidation state","ids": [379,380],"t": "In [AlCl(H2O)5]^2+: oxidation state of Al +3, covalency 6 (not the same).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Are the oxidation state and covalency of Al in [AlCl(H2O)5]^2+ same? No. The oxidation state of Al is +3 and the covalency is 6."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [369],"t": "The first element of groups 1, 2 and 13–17 (Li, Be, B to F) differs in many respects from the rest of its group.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "The first element of each of the groups 1 (lithium) and 2 (beryllium) and groups 13-17 (boron to fluorine) differs in many respects from the other members of their respective group."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [370],"t": "Li and Be form compounds with pronounced covalent character; other members of groups 1 and 2 form mainly ionic compounds.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "For example, lithium unlike other alkali metals, and beryllium unlike other alkaline earth metals, form compounds with pronounced covalent character; the other members of these groups predominantly form ionic compounds."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [371,372],"t": "Li resembles Mg and Be resembles Al: **diagonal relationship**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "In fact the behaviour of lithium and beryllium is more similar with the second element of the following group i.e., magnesium and aluminium, respectively. This sort of similarity is commonly referred to as diagonal relationship in the periodic properties."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [374],"t": "Anomaly due to small size, large charge/radius ratio, high electronegativity.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "The anomalous behaviour is attributed to their small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity of the elements."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [375],"t": "First member: only 4 valence orbitals (2s, 2p); second member: 9 (3s, 3p, 3d).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "In addition, the first member of group has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding, whereas the second member of the groups have nine valence orbitals (3s, 3p, 3d)."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [376],"t": "So **max covalency of the first member is 4**: B forms only [BF4]^–, but Al forms [AlF6]^3–.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "As a consequence of this, the maximum covalency of the first member of each group is 4 (e.g., boron can only form [BF4]^–, whereas the other members of the groups can expand their valence shell to accommodate more than four pairs of electrons e.g., aluminium [AlF6]^3– forms)."},{"sec": "Anomalous second period elements","ids": [377],"t": "First member of p-block forms pπ–pπ multiple bonds to itself (C=C, C≡C, N=N, N≡N) and to other period-2 elements (C=O, C=N, C≡N, N=O) much better than heavier members.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 20,"o": "Furthermore, the first member of p-block elements displays greater ability to form pπ – pπ multiple bonds to itself (e.g., C = C, C ≡ C, N = N, N ≡ N) and to other second period elements (e.g., C = O, C = N, C ≡ N, N = O) compared to subsequent members of the same group."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [384,385],"t": "Across a period radii decrease, so ∆iH increases (with exceptions) and ∆egH becomes more negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "The atomic and ionic radii, as we know, generally decrease in a period from left to right. As a consequence, the ionization enthalpies generally increase (with some exceptions as outlined in section 3.7.1(a)) and electron gain enthalpies become more negative across a period."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [386,387],"t": "Lowest ∆iH at the far left, most negative ∆egH at the far right, so reactivity is highest at the two extremes and lowest in the centre.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "The ionization enthalpy of the extreme left element in a period is the least and the electron gain enthalpy of the element on the extreme right is the highest negative. This results into high chemical reactivity at the two extremes and the lowest in the centre."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [388],"t": "Far left reacts by losing an electron (cation); far right by gaining one (anion).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Thus, the maximum chemical reactivity at the extreme left (among alkali metals) is exhibited by the loss of an electron leading to the formation of a cation and at the extreme right (among halogens) shown by the gain of an electron forming an anion."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [391],"t": "Metallic character falls and non-metallic rises from left to right.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Thus, the metallic character of an element, which is highest at the extremely left decreases and the non-metallic character increases while moving from left to right across the period."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [394],"t": "Elements at both ends of a period combine easily with oxygen.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Elements on two extremes of a period easily combine with oxygen to form oxides."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [395],"t": "Oxide of the far-left element is most basic (Na2O); far-right is most acidic (Cl2O7).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "The normal oxide formed by the element on extreme left is the most basic (e.g., Na2O), whereas that formed by the element on extreme right is the most acidic (e.g., Cl2O7)."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [396,397],"t": "Middle elements: amphoteric oxides (Al2O3, As2O3) or neutral (CO, NO, N2O). Amphoteric = acidic with bases, basic with acids.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Oxides of elements in the centre are amphoteric (e.g., Al2O3, As2O3) or neutral (e.g., CO, NO, N2O). Amphoteric oxides behave as acidic with bases and as basic with acids, whereas neutral oxides have no acidic or basic properties."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [398,399],"t": "Na2O + water gives a strong base; Cl2O7 + water gives a strong acid.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Problem 3.10 Show by a chemical reaction with water that Na2 O is a basic oxide and Cl2 O7 is an acidic oxide. Na2 O with water forms a strong base whereas Cl2 O7 forms strong acid."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [400],"t": "Na2O + H2O → 2NaOH; Cl2O7 + H2O → 2HClO4","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Na2O + H2O → 2NaOH; Cl2O7 + H2O → 2HClO4"},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [401,402],"t": "3d series: change in radius across a period is much smaller than for representative elements; even smaller for 4f.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "Among transition metals (3d series), the change in atomic radii is much smaller as compared to those of representative elements across the period. The change in atomic radii is still smaller among inner-transition metals (4f series)."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [403,404],"t": "Transition metals: ∆iH between s- and p-block, so less electropositive than groups 1 and 2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 21,"o": "The ionization enthalpies are intermediate between those of s- and p-blocks. As a consequence, they are less electropositive than group 1 and 2 metals."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [406,407],"t": "Down a main group: radii up, ∆iH down, ∆egH less negative (exceptions in period 3); metallic character increases.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 22,"o": "In a group, the increase in atomic and ionic radii with increase in atomic number generally results in a gradual decrease in ionization enthalpies and a regular decrease (with exception in some third period elements as shown in section 3.7.1(d)) in electron gain enthalpies in the case of main group elements. Thus, the metallic character increases down the group and non-metallic character decreases."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [409],"t": "**Transition elements show the reverse trend down a group.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 22,"o": "In the case of transition elements, however, a reverse trend is observed."},{"sec": "Periodic trends and chemical reactivity","ids": [410],"t": "Explained by atomic size and ionization enthalpy.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "per","page": 22,"o": "This can be explained in terms of atomic size and ionization enthalpy."}]);
})();
